  <div class="col" style="margin-top:14px">
    <h3>Try this</h3>
    <ul class="tight">
      <li><em>The wall of the exercise</em> has a wind of 1.5 kN/m on a wall 6 m tall, which is 9 kN in all. The prop is pressed with 11.25 kN, more than the whole wind, because only three fifths of its force act sideways. Three fifths of 11.25 kN is 6.75 kN, which is how hard the prop pushes the wall back, and the floor holds the last 2.25 kN at the foot of the wall.</li>
      <li>Stay there and look at the upward forces. The prop also pushes the wall up, with 9 kN, so the floor carries only 11 of the 20 kN that the wall weighs. The other 9 kN go down through the prop to its foot.</li>
      <li>Drag <em>Height of the grip</em> down from 4 m to 3 m. The sideways force at the foot of the wall falls to exactly 0, because the prop now grips the wall at the height where the whole push of the wind acts, and so it takes all of it.</li>
      <li><em>Grip low on the wall</em> moves the grip down to 2 m. The prop is pressed with 16.22 kN, and its sideways push of 13.5 kN is larger than the wind, so the floor has to hold the foot of the wall the other way, with −4.5 kN. The upward force at the foot of the prop is still 9 kN. The wall is bent four times as hard as in the exercise, with 12 kN·m at the grip, and the curve now lies on the far side all the way from the foot to the top.</li>
      <li><em>Grip at the top</em> lets the prop and the foot of the wall share the wind evenly, with 4.5 kN each. The wall is now bent like a beam on two supports, with 6.75 kN·m at half its height and nothing at the grip.</li>
      <li><em>Steep prop</em> moves the foot of the prop in to 1.5 m. The sideways forces are the same as in the exercise, but the prop is pressed with 19.22 kN and lifts the wall with 18 kN, so only 2 kN are left on the foot of the wall. Raise <em>Wind on the wall</em> one step, to 1.75 kN/m, and the pill turns red, because the prop then lifts harder than the wall weighs.</li>
      <li><em>Wind from the other side</em> turns every force from the wind around. The prop is pulled with 11.25 kN and the pill turns amber. The floor must now hold the foot of the prop down with 9 kN, and the wall presses on the floor with 29 kN.</li>
      <li>Press <em>Reset all</em> and then drag the wind from 1.5 to 3 kN/m. The line under the tiles then reads prop force +100 %, sideways at D +100 %, upward at D +100 %. Every force from the wind doubles with the wind, but the weight of the wall does not, so the upward force at the foot of the wall falls from 11 to 2 kN.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
